[EXAMPLES]
overview: Real-world examples of documentation updates
purpose: Show how to apply self-learning process

[EXAMPLE_1_USER_EXPLICIT_RULE]
scenario: User says "Never use std::cout in core, use engine_log.h"

step_1: Identify
  - This is from user (meets CRITERIA_1)
  - It's a constraint/rule
  - Target: CLAUDE.md

step_2: Locate
  - Open /CLAUDE.md
  - Find section "⚠️ Critical Rules"
  - Add as critical rule

step_3: Check duplicate
  - Search CLAUDE.md for "cout" or "engine_log"
  - Not found, proceed

step_4: Write
  - Never use std::cout/cin/cerr, use engine_log.h
  - Why: engine_log.h provides formatted, filterable logging
  - How: #include "engine_log.h" and use the macros

step_5: Edit
  Use Edit tool to add to CLAUDE.md

step_6: Verify
  - CLAUDE.md < 500 words
  - No syntax errors
  - Rule is clear

step_7: Commit
  git commit -m "docs: [rule] add logging requirement

  Source: User explicit statement
  File: CLAUDE.md

  Co-Authored-By: Claude Haiku 4.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"

[EXAMPLE_2_FOUND_IN_CODE]
scenario: Found in render_packet.cpp: "// Never store frame_allocator pointers across frames"

step_1: Identify
  - Found in code with comment (meets CRITERIA_2)
  - It's a memory/architecture constraint
  - Target: CONSTRAINTS.toon

step_2: Locate
  - Open docs/agent/CONSTRAINTS.toon
  - Find [MEMORY_CONSTRAINTS] section
  - Add details there

step_3: Check duplicate
  - Search "frame" in CONSTRAINTS.toon
  - Found existing info about frame_allocator
  - Update existing section instead of adding new

step_4: Write
  - Add: "Never store pointers from frame_allocator across frames"
  - Why: Frame allocator resets at each frame start
  - How: Always transfer data or use other allocators for persistence

step_5: Edit
  Update CONSTRAINTS.toon frame_allocator section

step_6: Verify
  - Info integrates with existing section
  - References actual code location
  - Clear and concise

step_7: Commit
  git commit -m "docs: [constraint] clarify frame allocator lifespan

  Source: Code inspection, render_packet.cpp
  File: docs/agent/CONSTRAINTS.toon

  Co-Authored-By: Claude Haiku 4.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"

[EXAMPLE_3_DISCOVERED_PATTERN]
scenario: Found pattern: every system inherits from ecs::system_interface

step_1: Identify
  - Discovered from code analysis (meets CRITERIA_3)
  - It's an architectural pattern
  - Target: ARCHITECTURE.toon or CODE_CONVENTIONS.toon

step_2: Locate
  - Open docs/agent/CODE_CONVENTIONS.toon
  - Find [SYSTEM_PATTERNS] section

step_3: Check duplicate
  - Search "system_interface" in existing docs
  - Found reference in ARCHITECTURE.toon
  - Will add CODE_CONVENTIONS perspective

step_4: Write
  In [SYSTEM_PATTERNS] section:
  pattern: All systems inherit from ecs::system_interface
  location: Core/core/ecs/ecs_system.h
  how: Override initialize(), update(), shutdown()
  example: See Engine/scene/scn_renderer_system.cpp

step_5: Edit
  Update CODE_CONVENTIONS.toon

step_6: Verify
  - Doesn't duplicate ARCHITECTURE.toon
  - Adds code-level pattern guidance
  - References actual files

step_7: Commit
  git commit -m "docs: [conventions] document system inheritance pattern

  Source: Code discovery across systems
  File: docs/agent/CODE_CONVENTIONS.toon

  Co-Authored-By: Claude Haiku 4.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"

[EXAMPLE_4_FIXING_ERROR]
scenario: ARCHITECTURE.toon says "ECS supports 5 worlds" but code comment says "8 worlds max"

step_1: Identify
  - Found error in docs (meets CRITERIA_4)
  - Target: ARCHITECTURE.toon (ECS section)

step_2: Locate
  - Open docs/agent/ARCHITECTURE.toon
  - Find [ECS_WORLD_MANAGEMENT] section

step_3: Check duplicate
  - This is the only entry for world limits
  - Proceed

step_4: Write
  Change: "Supports up to 5 independent worlds"
  To: "Supports up to 8 independent worlds (world_salt + bind_res)"
  And verify: Check code for actual limit

step_5: Edit
  Update ARCHITECTURE.toon

step_6: Verify
  - Number matches code comment
  - Other docs that reference this are still valid

step_7: Commit
  git commit -m "docs: [fix] correct ECS world count limit

  Source: Code verification, ecs_world.hpp comment line 47
  File: docs/agent/ARCHITECTURE.toon

  Co-Authored-By: Claude Haiku 4.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>"

[WHAT_TO_AVOID]
example_bad_1: Too vague
  "The system is complex"
  problem: No actionable information

example_bad_2: Speculation
  "Probably the developers meant to implement it this way"
  problem: Vague, unverified

example_bad_3: Opinion
  "This would be better if implemented differently"
  problem: Not a rule, just preference

example_bad_4: Obvious
  "The code iterates through elements"
  problem: Anyone reading code sees this

example_bad_5: Implementation detail
  "Vector grows by 2x when capacity exceeded"
  problem: Can change, not architectural rule

[KEY_TAKEAWAYS]
✅ Small targeted updates (5-10 lines)
✅ One file per update (don't scatter info)
✅ Always verify source (code review or user confirmation)
✅ Link to references (line numbers, existing docs)
✅ Clear commit message with source attribution
✅ Check for duplicates before adding

❌ Large multi-concept updates
❌ Speculative or opinion-based info
❌ Obvious facts from code
❌ Without source attribution
❌ Breaking existing structure/format
❌ Forgetting to commit [docs] prefix
